#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161846 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "6276f196-80de-58b7-acb6-a715ee955f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9073" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${2x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y =1-3X$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y$, where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X$, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X$, where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X$, respectively.\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$.\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = 1-3X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$. Moreover, for $0 < x < 1$, we have $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bb641b1920d0ded33e4ffa25f3e0af856550dd3820edf30416bd79c3a6da264.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(1 - 3X \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(-3X \\le y - 1) \\\\\n&= P(X \\ge \\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= 1 - F_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$, we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 - (-\\frac{1}{3}) \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}(1 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{2}{9}(1 - y),\\begin{vmatrix}-2 < y < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161847 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b9f862ab-0544-503e-8627-3de73ec60594" :question/math-academy-id "e-9074" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${\\frac{3}{8}(2 - x)^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y = (2-X)^3$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y$, where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X$, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X$, where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X$, respectively.\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$.\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = (2-X)^3$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$. Moreover, for $0 \\lt x \\lt 2$, we have $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$.\n\n![](images/2c/2c07ffe5af282ba1689cb6f49201b979afb4186d0128dd6a97f446b7240d76d6.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P((2 - X)^{3} \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(2 - X \\le y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= P(-X \\le y^{1/3} - 2) \\\\\n&= P(X \\ge 2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= 1 - F_{X}(2 - y^{1/3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$, we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 - (-\\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}}) \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot f_{X}(2 - y^{1/3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot f_{X}(2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(2 - (2 - y^{1/3}))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(2 - 2 + y^{1/3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(y^{1/3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{8},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < y < 8 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a39d6df3-4e4a-5932-a1af-4e0f960c190a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {8e^{-8y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158691} {:answer/id #uuid "b0871774-b1e2-5596-8d00-8ff73ed2f061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {2e^{-2y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158692} {:answer/id #uuid "bec17b16-7ddb-518e-b615-9860111fb70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{1}{2}e^{-4y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158693} {:answer/id #uuid "d263f88d-7fa5-5478-a3a2-09700c40f738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {2e^{-4y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158694} {:answer/id #uuid "7cde2501-ad66-5893-b560-ce623dbaff40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{1}{4}e^{-4y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158695}] :answer-field/id #uuid "64902e3c-53ad-5d5a-a122-1bd9b485f300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158690}] :question/id #uuid "80402a14-4dfc-5a15-8269-ddd0f957785c" :question/math-academy-id "q-108501" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n$f_{X}(x) = {4e^{-4x},\\begin{vmatrix}x > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y = 2X$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?"}]}
